# SYNOPSIS

```usage
mv fromfile tofile
mv fromfile ... todir
```

# DESCRIPTION

`mv` moves <fromfile> to <tofile>, or each <fromfile> into the directory <todir> under its own name.
A <tofile> that exists is replaced.
Two directories given alone is a rename of the first to the second's name.

Within one directory, a move is a rename.
Between directories of one volume that can rename across them, it is that volume's rename.
Otherwise a file is copied, its time kept where the target allows, and the original removed; a directory can't be moved that way.

# SEE ALSO

cp(1), rm(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

The exit string is `bad usage` for too few names or several moved to what isn't a directory, `failure` if a move failed, and `mv` if a target couldn't be removed to make way, after which nothing more is moved.
